\details{
Wraps the ESPN core-v2 endpoint
\code{events/{game_id}/competitions/{game_id}/competitors/{team_id}/statistics}.
This wrapper reads the teams list for the game, fetches the team
statistics resource for \strong{both} teams, and walks the per-category
\code{splits -> categories -> athletes} arrays -- the per-player box-score
tree that the team-level \code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_team_statistics]{espn_cfb_game_team_statistics()}} wrapper
(which walks \code{categories -> stats}) drops entirely.

Each athlete entry under a category carries only a \verb{$ref} to that
athlete's per-game statistics resource; this wrapper dereferences each
unique athlete statistics \verb{$ref} and flattens the nested
\code{splits -> categories -> stats} tree into one long frame: one row per
(team x athlete x category x stat). Athlete display names and position
ids are joined from \code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_team_roster]{espn_cfb_game_team_roster()}}; a roster failure
degrades to \code{NA} names rather than erroring. The long shape absorbs
ESPN's habit of adding and retiring stat keys across seasons.

When \code{position_detail = TRUE} (the default) the ESPN position catalog
(\code{\link[=espn_cfb_positions]{espn_cfb_positions()}}) is fetched once and joined onto \code{position_id},
so the output carries the full position name / abbreviation (see
\emph{Details}).

When \code{position_detail = TRUE} (the default), the \code{position_id} column
(roster-joined per athlete) is enriched with five columns from the ESPN
position catalog (\code{\link[=espn_cfb_positions]{espn_cfb_positions()}}): \code{position_name},
\code{position_display_name}, \code{position_abbreviation}, \code{position_leaf}, and
\code{position_parent_id}. The catalog is fetched once per call. A row whose
\code{position_id} is missing or unmatched receives \code{NA} for all five, and a
catalog-fetch failure degrades the whole set to \code{NA} rather than
erroring the wrapper. With \code{position_detail = FALSE} the five columns
(and the catalog fetch) are skipped.
}
